---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import './WhatisForex.css';
---

<Layout title="What is Forex? - NFX Learning">
  
  <main class="page-container">
    
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- HERO SECTION - COMPACT, NO OVERLAP          -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <div class="hero">
      <div class="hero-badge">The World's Largest Financial Market</div>
      <h1 class="hero-title">What is <span class="hero-highlight">Forex</span>?</h1>
      <p class="hero-subtitle">A complete deep-dive into the foreign exchange market — how it works, who trades it, and why it's the most liquid market on Earth.</p>
      
      <div class="hero-stats">
        <div class="hero-stat">
          <div class="hero-stat-value">$6.6 Trillion</div>
          <div class="hero-stat-label">Daily Trading Volume</div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-stat">
          <div class="hero-stat-value">24/5</div>
          <div class="hero-stat-label">Hours Open</div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-stat">
          <div class="hero-stat-value">180+</div>
          <div class="hero-stat-label">Currencies</div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-stat">
          <div class="hero-stat-value">$10+ Trillion</div>
          <div class="hero-stat-label">Derivatives Market</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- MAIN CONTENT - EVERYTHING VISIBLE            -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <div class="content-wrapper">
      
      <!-- SECTION 1: DEEP DEFINITION -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">01</span>
          <h2 class="section-title">The Foreign Exchange Market Explained</h2>
        </div>
        
        <div class="definition-card">
          <p class="definition-text">The <strong>foreign exchange market (Forex, FX, or currency market)</strong> is a global decentralized or over-the-counter (OTC) market for the trading of currencies. This market determines foreign exchange rates for every currency. It includes all aspects of buying, selling and exchanging currencies at current or determined prices.</p>
          <p class="definition-text">Unlike stock markets which have a physical location (like the New York Stock Exchange), Forex has no central exchange. It operates through a global network of banks, brokers, and financial institutions. This means no single entity can manipulate the market, and trading can happen anywhere with an internet connection.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="info-grid">
          <div class="info-card">
            <div class="info-icon">🌍</div>
            <h3>Decentralized Nature</h3>
            <p>Forex operates through a global network of banks, institutions, and brokers. There is no single central exchange. Trading happens electronically over-the-counter (OTC) through computer networks between traders around the world.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="info-card">
            <div class="info-icon">💧</div>
            <h3>Unmatched Liquidity</h3>
            <p>With over $6.6 trillion traded daily, Forex is the most liquid market in the world. This means you can enter and exit positions instantly at transparent prices with minimal slippage, even with large position sizes.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="info-card">
            <div class="info-icon">⏰</div>
            <h3>24/5 Accessibility</h3>
            <p>The Forex market opens Sunday at 5 PM EST and closes Friday at 5 PM EST. Trading happens continuously across global financial centers: Sydney, Tokyo, London, and New York, allowing traders to respond to news anytime.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="info-card">
            <div class="info-icon">📈</div>
            <h3>Leverage Trading</h3>
            <p>Forex brokers offer significant leverage (50:1, 100:1, even 500:1), allowing traders to control large positions with small capital. While this amplifies profits, it equally magnifies potential losses.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 2: HOW CURRENCY TRADING WORKS -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">02</span>
          <h2 class="section-title">How Currency Trading Works</h2>
        </div>
        
        <p class="lead-text">Forex trading always involves trading currency pairs. When you buy one currency, you're simultaneously selling another. The goal is to profit from changes in exchange rates.</p>
        
        <div class="example-box-large">
          <div class="example-title">
            <span class="pair-code">EUR/USD</span>
            <span class="pair-name">Euro vs US Dollar</span>
          </div>
          
          <div class="example-scenarios">
            <div class="scenario buy-scenario">
              <div class="scenario-header">
                <span class="scenario-badge buy">BUY</span>
                <span class="scenario-label">Bullish on Euro</span>
              </div>
              <p>If you believe the Euro will strengthen against the US Dollar, you would <strong>BUY EUR/USD</strong>. You are buying Euros and selling Dollars simultaneously.</p>
              <div class="example-calculation">
                <div class="calc-row">Buy at: <strong>1.0925</strong></div>
                <div class="calc-row">Sell at: <strong>1.0950</strong></div>
                <div class="calc-row profit">Profit: +25 pips (+$250 per standard lot)</div>
              </div>
            </div>
            
            <div class="scenario sell-scenario">
              <div class="scenario-header">
                <span class="scenario-badge sell">SELL</span>
                <span class="scenario-label">Bearish on Euro</span>
              </div>
              <p>If you believe the Euro will weaken against the US Dollar, you would <strong>SELL EUR/USD</strong>. You are selling Euros and buying Dollars simultaneously.</p>
              <div class="example-calculation">
                <div class="calc-row">Sell at: <strong>1.0925</strong></div>
                <div class="calc-row">Buy at: <strong>1.0900</strong></div>
                <div class="calc-row profit">Profit: +25 pips (+$250 per standard lot)</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="example-note">
            <strong>💡 Key Concept:</strong> Every Forex trade is a simultaneous buy and sell. You are always long one currency and short another. The profit or loss depends on the direction of the exchange rate movement.
          </div>
        </div>
        
        <div class="warning-box">
          <div class="warning-icon">⚠️</div>
          <div class="warning-content">
            <strong>Important Risk Disclosure:</strong> Forex trading involves significant risk of loss. Most retail trader accounts lose money when trading Forex. Never risk more than 1-2% of your account on a single trade. Always use proper risk management including stop-loss orders.
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 3: ALL CURRENCY PAIRS - COMPLETE LIST -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">03</span>
          <h2 class="section-title">Currency Pairs Classification</h2>
        </div>
        
        <div class="pair-categories">
          <div class="category-card majors">
            <div class="category-header">
              <span class="category-icon">⭐</span>
              <h3>Major Pairs</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Always include USD on one side. Most liquid, tightest spreads (0.1-2 pips). Account for 80%+ of daily volume.</p>
            <div class="pair-list">
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">EUR/USD</span>
                <span class="pair-info">Euro / US Dollar — "Fiber"</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">0.1-0.3 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">USD/JPY</span>
                <span class="pair-info">US Dollar / Japanese Yen — "Gopher"</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">0.1-0.3 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">GBP/USD</span>
                <span class="pair-info">British Pound / US Dollar — "Cable"</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">0.5-1 pip</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">USD/CHF</span>
                <span class="pair-info">US Dollar / Swiss Franc — "Swissie"</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">1-2 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">AUD/USD</span>
                <span class="pair-info">Australian Dollar / US Dollar — "Aussie"</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">0.5-1 pip</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">USD/CAD</span>
                <span class="pair-info">US Dollar / Canadian Dollar — "Loonie"</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">1-2 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">NZD/USD</span>
                <span class="pair-info">New Zealand Dollar / US Dollar — "Kiwi"</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">1-2 pips</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="category-card minors">
            <div class="category-header">
              <span class="category-icon">🪙</span>
              <h3>Minor Pairs (Crosses)</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Cross-currency pairs that don't include USD. Wider spreads (2-10 pips). Less liquid but still tradable.</p>
            <div class="pair-list">
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">EUR/GBP</span>
                <span class="pair-info">Euro / British Pound</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">1-2 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">EUR/JPY</span>
                <span class="pair-info">Euro / Japanese Yen</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">1-3 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">GBP/JPY</span>
                <span class="pair-info">British Pound / Japanese Yen — "Dragon"</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">2-5 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">AUD/JPY</span>
                <span class="pair-info">Australian Dollar / Japanese Yen</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">1-3 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">EUR/AUD</span>
                <span class="pair-info">Euro / Australian Dollar</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">2-4 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">GBP/AUD</span>
                <span class="pair-info">British Pound / Australian Dollar</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">3-6 pips</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="category-card exotics">
            <div class="category-header">
              <span class="category-icon">🌍</span>
              <h3>Exotic Pairs</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Major currency vs emerging economy currency. Very wide spreads (10-100+ pips). High volatility, less liquid.</p>
            <div class="pair-list">
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">USD/TRY</span>
                <span class="pair-info">US Dollar / Turkish Lira</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">20-50 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">USD/ZAR</span>
                <span class="pair-info">US Dollar / South African Rand</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">30-60 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">USD/MXN</span>
                <span class="pair-info">US Dollar / Mexican Peso</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">20-40 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">USD/SGD</span>
                <span class="pair-info">US Dollar / Singapore Dollar</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">5-10 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">USD/HKD</span>
                <span class="pair-info">US Dollar / Hong Kong Dollar</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">5-15 pips</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pair-item">
                <span class="pair-symbol">EUR/TRY</span>
                <span class="pair-info">Euro / Turkish Lira</span>
                <span class="pair-spread">30-80 pips</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 4: DEEP TERMINOLOGY -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">04</span>
          <h2 class="section-title">Essential Forex Terminology (Deep Dive)</h2>
        </div>
        
        <div class="terminology-grid-advanced">
          <div class="term-card-advanced">
            <div class="term-header">
              <span class="term-name">Pip</span>
              <span class="term-abbrev">Percentage in Point</span>
            </div>
            <div class="term-definition-deep">
              <p>The smallest price movement in a currency pair. For most pairs, one pip = 0.0001 (1/100th of 1%). For JPY pairs, one pip = 0.01 (1/100th of 1 Yen).</p>
              <div class="term-example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong> EUR/USD moves from 1.1050 to 1.1051 = +1 pip<br>
                <strong>Value:</strong> $10 per standard lot (100,000 units)<br>
                <strong>Value:</strong> $1 per mini lot (10,000 units)<br>
                <strong>Value:</strong> $0.10 per micro lot (1,000 units)
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="term-card-advanced">
            <div class="term-header">
              <span class="term-name">Spread</span>
              <span class="term-abbrev">Bid/Ask Difference</span>
            </div>
            <div class="term-definition-deep">
              <p>The difference between the Bid price (sell) and Ask price (buy). This is how brokers make money when no commission is charged.</p>
              <div class="term-example-box">
                <strong>Formula:</strong> Ask Price - Bid Price = Spread<br>
                <strong>Example:</strong> Bid 1.1050 / Ask 1.1052 = 2 pip spread<br>
                <strong>Cost:</strong> You pay the spread when entering and exiting trades
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="term-card-advanced">
            <div class="term-header">
              <span class="term-name">Leverage</span>
              <span class="term-abbrev">Borrowed Capital</span>
            </div>
            <div class="term-definition-deep">
              <p>Using borrowed money to control larger positions than your account balance would normally allow. Amplifies both profits and losses.</p>
              <div class="term-example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong> 100:1 leverage means $1,000 controls $100,000<br>
                <strong>Common ratios:</strong> 50:1, 100:1, 200:1, 500:1<br>
                <strong>Risk:</strong> A 1% move against you = 100% loss at 100:1 leverage
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="term-card-advanced">
            <div class="term-header">
              <span class="term-name">Margin</span>
              <span class="term-abbrev">Collateral Required</span>
            </div>
            <div class="term-definition-deep">
              <p>The amount of money required to open and maintain a leveraged position. Not a fee, but a deposit held by your broker.</p>
              <div class="term-example-box">
                <strong>Formula:</strong> Position Size ÷ Leverage = Margin Required<br>
                <strong>Example:</strong> $100,000 position ÷ 100:1 = $1,000 margin<br>
                <strong>Margin Call:</strong> When account equity falls below required margin
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="term-card-advanced">
            <div class="term-header">
              <span class="term-name">Lot Size</span>
              <span class="term-abbrev">Trade Volume</span>
            </div>
            <div class="term-definition-deep">
              <p>Standardized trading volumes used in Forex. Brokers allow trading in fractions of lots.</p>
              <div class="term-example-box">
                <strong>Standard Lot:</strong> 100,000 units = $10/pip<br>
                <strong>Mini Lot:</strong> 10,000 units = $1/pip<br>
                <strong>Micro Lot:</strong> 1,000 units = $0.10/pip<br>
                <strong>Nano Lot:</strong> 100 units = $0.01/pip
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="term-card-advanced">
            <div class="term-header">
              <span class="term-name">Swap/Rollover</span>
              <span class="term-abbrev">Overnight Interest</span>
            </div>
            <div class="term-definition-deep">
              <p>Interest paid or earned for holding positions overnight, based on the interest rate differential between the two currencies.</p>
              <div class="term-example-box">
                <strong>Positive Swap:</strong> Earn interest (buy high-yield, sell low-yield)<br>
                <strong>Negative Swap:</strong> Pay interest<br>
                <strong>Triple Swap:</strong> Wednesday night = 3x interest for weekend
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 5: MARKET PARTICIPANTS -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">05</span>
          <h2 class="section-title">Who Participates in the Forex Market?</h2>
        </div>
        
        <div class="participants-showcase">
          <div class="participant-card-large">
            <div class="participant-icon">🏦</div>
            <div class="participant-info">
              <h3>Central Banks</h3>
              <p>The most powerful participants. Control monetary policy, interest rates, and currency reserves. They can intentionally weaken or strengthen their currency through intervention.</p>
              <div class="participant-example">Examples: Federal Reserve (FED), European Central Bank (ECB), Bank of Japan (BOJ), Bank of England (BOE)</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="participant-card-large">
            <div class="participant-icon">🏢</div>
            <div class="participant-info">
              <h3>Commercial Banks</h3>
              <p>Handle transactions for clients and trade on their own behalf (proprietary trading). Account for the majority of daily trading volume.</p>
              <div class="participant-example">Examples: JPMorgan Chase, Deutsche Bank, Citibank, Barclays, UBS</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="participant-card-large">
            <div class="participant-icon">🏭</div>
            <div class="participant-info">
              <h3>Corporations & Businesses</h3>
              <p>Hedge against currency risk from international operations. They need to convert profits, pay suppliers, or receive payments in foreign currencies.</p>
              <div class="participant-example">Examples: Apple, Toyota, Nestlé, Samsung — companies with global operations</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="participant-card-large">
            <div class="participant-icon">💰</div>
            <div class="participant-info">
              <h3>Hedge Funds & Investment Managers</h3>
              <p>Speculate on currency movements using leverage and complex strategies. Manage large portfolios and seek alpha through currency exposure.</p>
              <div class="participant-example">Examples: Bridgewater Associates, Renaissance Technologies, Soros Fund Management</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="participant-card-large">
            <div class="participant-icon">👤</div>
            <div class="participant-info">
              <h3>Retail Traders</h3>
              <p>Individual traders like you, trading through online brokers. Access the market with small accounts using leverage. The fastest-growing segment of Forex participants.</p>
              <div class="participant-example">Examples: Independent traders using platforms like MetaTrader, cTrader, or proprietary broker platforms</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6: TRADING SESSIONS -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">06</span>
          <h2 class="section-title">Forex Trading Sessions (24-Hour Market)</h2>
        </div>
        
        <div class="sessions-showcase">
          <div class="session-card">
            <div class="session-timezone">🇦🇺 Sydney Session</div>
            <div class="session-hours">10 PM - 7 AM EST</div>
            <div class="session-details">
              <span class="volatility low">Low Volatility</span>
              <span class="focus">Focus: AUD, NZD, JPY</span>
            </div>
            <div class="session-desc">The first session to open each week. Sets the tone but generally has the lowest volatility.</div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="session-card">
            <div class="session-timezone">🇯🇵 Tokyo Session</div>
            <div class="session-hours">7 PM - 4 AM EST</div>
            <div class="session-details">
              <span class="volatility moderate">Moderate Volatility</span>
              <span class="focus">Focus: JPY, AUD, NZD</span>
            </div>
            <div class="session-desc">Asian session where Yen pairs see most movement. Known for range-bound trading.</div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="session-card">
            <div class="session-timezone">🇬🇧 London Session</div>
            <div class="session-hours">3 AM - 12 PM EST</div>
            <div class="session-details">
              <span class="volatility high">Highest Volatility</span>
              <span class="focus">Focus: All pairs, especially EUR, GBP</span>
            </div>
            <div class="session-desc">The most liquid session. 35-40% of daily volume. Best for trending strategies.</div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="session-card">
            <div class="session-timezone">🇺🇸 New York Session</div>
            <div class="session-hours">8 AM - 5 PM EST</div>
            <div class="session-details">
              <span class="volatility high">High Volatility</span>
              <span class="focus">Focus: USD pairs, all majors</span>
            </div>
            <div class="session-desc">Major economic news releases. High liquidity, especially during London overlap.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        
        <div class="overlap-highlight">
          <div class="overlap-title">⏰ Critical Overlap Periods (Best Trading Times)</div>
          <div class="overlap-items">
            <div class="overlap-item">
              <strong>London/New York Overlap:</strong> 8 AM - 12 PM EST — The most active trading period with highest liquidity and tightest spreads.
            </div>
            <div class="overlap-item">
              <strong>Tokyo/London Overlap:</strong> 3 AM - 4 AM EST — Good for Yen pairs during the crossover.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 7: PRICE DRIVERS -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">07</span>
          <h2 class="section-title">What Drives Currency Prices?</h2>
        </div>
        
        <div class="drivers-grid">
          <div class="driver-card">
            <div class="driver-icon">📊</div>
            <h3>Interest Rates</h3>
            <p>Higher interest rates attract foreign capital, strengthening the currency. Central bank decisions (FED, ECB, BOJ, BOE) are the most important fundamental drivers. When a central bank raises rates, the currency typically appreciates. When they cut rates, it typically depreciates.</p>
            <div class="driver-example">Example: When FED raises rates, USD strengthens against other currencies.</div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="driver-card">
            <div class="driver-icon">📈</div>
            <h3>Economic Data Releases</h3>
            <p>Major economic indicators move markets instantly. Better than expected = currency strengthens. Worse than expected = currency weakens. Traders watch these releases closely and volatility spikes around announcement times.</p>
            <div class="driver-example">Key Indicators: Non-Farm Payrolls (NFP), CPI (Inflation), GDP, PMI, Retail Sales, Unemployment Claims</div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="driver-card">
            <div class="driver-icon">🌍</div>
            <h3>Geopolitical Events</h3>
            <p>Elections, wars, trade disputes, natural disasters, and political instability cause currency volatility. Safe-haven currencies (USD, CHF, JPY, Gold) rise during uncertainty. Risk currencies (AUD, NZD, CAD, emerging markets) fall during turmoil.</p>
            <div class="driver-example">Example: During the Russia-Ukraine war, USD and CHF strengthened while EUR weakened significantly.</div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="driver-card">
            <div class="driver-icon">🏛️</div>
            <h3>Central Bank Policy</h3>
            <p>Quantitative Easing (QE) weakens currency by increasing money supply. Quantitative Tightening (QT) strengthens currency by reducing money supply. Forward guidance (signaling future policy) impacts expectations before actual changes happen.</p>
            <div class="driver-example">Example: When FED announced QE in 2020, USD weakened. When they announced tapering, USD strengthened.</div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="driver-card">
            <div class="driver-icon">📉</div>
            <h3>Market Sentiment & Risk Appetite</h3>
            <p>Risk-On sentiment: Traders buy high-yielding currencies (AUD, NZD, CAD, emerging markets). Risk-Off sentiment: Traders buy safe havens (USD, JPY, CHF, Gold). The COT (Commitment of Traders) report shows institutional positioning.</p>
            <div class="driver-example">Example: During stock market rallies, AUD and NZD typically rise. During crashes, JPY and USD rise.</div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="driver-card">
            <div class="driver-icon">⚡</div>
            <h3>Technical Factors</h3>
            <p>Support/resistance levels, moving averages, trend lines, chart patterns, and Fibonacci levels become self-fulfilling prophecies as many traders react to the same technical signals. Order flow and liquidity levels also matter.</p>
            <div class="driver-example">Example: When EUR/USD hits a major support level, many traders buy, causing the price to bounce.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 8: TRADING STYLES -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">08</span>
          <h2 class="section-title">Forex Trading Styles & Timeframes</h2>
        </div>
        
        <div class="styles-grid">
          <div class="style-card">
            <div class="style-name">Scalping</div>
            <div class="style-timeframe">Seconds to Minutes</div>
            <div class="style-desc">Ultra-short-term trading aiming for 5-10 pips per trade. Requires low spreads, fast execution, and intense focus. 50-200+ trades per day.</div>
            <div class="style-requirements">Requirements: Low spreads, fast platform, high concentration, strong discipline</div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="style-card">
            <div class="style-name">Day Trading</div>
            <div class="style-timeframe">Minutes to Hours</div>
            <div class="style-desc">Open and close positions within the same day. No overnight risk. Aim for 20-50 pips per trade. Focus on technical analysis and session volatility.</div>
            <div class="style-requirements">Requirements: Technical analysis skills, session awareness, risk management</div>
