      <!-- SECTION 9: COMPLETE RISK MANAGEMENT -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">09</span>
          <h2 class="section-title">Essential Risk Management Rules</h2>
        </div>
        
        <div class="risk-management-system">
          <div class="risk-rule-detailed">
            <div class="rule-number-large">1</div>
            <div class="rule-content-detailed">
              <h3>Never Risk More Than 1-2% Per Trade</h3>
              <p>This is the golden rule of trading. If you have a $10,000 account, you should risk no more than $100-200 on any single trade. This ensures you can survive a losing streak of 10-20 trades in a row.</p>
              <div class="rule-formula">Position Size = (Account Risk % × Account Balance) ÷ (Stop Loss in Pips × Pip Value)</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="risk-rule-detailed">
            <div class="rule-number-large">2</div>
            <div class="rule-content-detailed">
              <h3>Always Use Stop Loss Orders</h3>
              <p>Predetermine your exit before entering any trade. A stop loss is an automatic order that closes your trade at a specified price, preventing unlimited losses. Never move your stop loss further away once placed.</p>
              <div class="rule-formula">Stop Loss Placement = Based on technical levels (support/resistance, ATR, volatility)</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="risk-rule-detailed">
            <div class="rule-number-large">3</div>
            <div class="rule-content-detailed">
              <h3>Maintain a Minimum 1:2 Risk/Reward Ratio</h3>
              <p>For every $1 you risk, aim to make $2. This means you can be wrong 50% of the time and still be profitable. A 1:2 ratio requires your profit target to be twice your stop loss distance.</p>
              <div class="rule-formula">Example: Risk 20 pips → Target 40+ pips. Risk $100 → Target $200+</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="risk-rule-detailed">
            <div class="rule-number-large">4</div>
            <div class="rule-content-detailed">
              <h3>Calculate Position Size Correctly</h3>
              <p>Position size determines how much you risk per pip. For a 20-pip stop loss and $100 risk, you need $5 per pip. This means trading 0.5 standard lots (50,000 units) at $10/pip standard.</p>
              <div class="rule-formula">Position Size (lots) = Risk Amount ÷ (Stop Loss in Pips × Pip Value per Lot)</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="risk-rule-detailed">
            <div class="rule-number-large">5</div>
            <div class="rule-content-detailed">
              <h3>Understand Currency Correlations</h3>
              <p>EUR/USD and GBP/USD often move together (positive correlation). EUR/USD and USD/CHF move opposite (negative correlation). Trading correlated pairs doubles your risk without realizing it.</p>
              <div class="rule-formula">Positive Correlation (+0.80+): EUR/USD & GBP/USD, AUD/USD & NZD/USD<br>Negative Correlation (-0.80+): EUR/USD & USD/CHF, GBP/USD & USD/JPY</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="risk-rule-detailed">
            <div class="rule-number-large">6</div>
            <div class="rule-content-detailed">
              <h3>Keep a Detailed Trading Journal</h3>
              <p>Track every single trade: entry price, exit price, stop loss, target, reason for entry, emotions during trade, screenshot of chart, and lessons learned. Review weekly to identify patterns in your winning and losing trades.</p>
              <div class="rule-formula">Journal Fields: Date, Pair, Direction, Entry, Exit, Pips, Risk/Reward, Setup, Emotion, Screenshot</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 10: ADVANTAGES & DISADVANTAGES -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <span class="section-number">10</span>
          <h2 class="section-title">Forex Market: Advantages & Disadvantages</h2>
        </div>
        
        <div class="pros-cons-grid">
          <div class="pros-card">
            <div class="pros-cons-header">✅ Advantages of Forex Trading</div>
            <ul class="pros-cons-list">
              <li><strong>Highest Liquidity:</strong> $6.6 trillion daily volume means instant execution with minimal slippage</li>
              <li><strong>24/5 Accessibility:</strong> Trade anytime, anywhere. Perfect for part-time traders with day jobs</li>
              <li><strong>Low Transaction Costs:</strong> Tight spreads (0.1-2 pips on majors) and often zero commission</li>
              <li><strong>High Leverage:</strong> Control large positions with small capital (50:1, 100:1, even 500:1)</li>
              <li><strong>No Central Exchange:</strong> No single entity can manipulate the market or halt trading</li>
              <li><strong>Trade Both Directions:</strong> Profit in rising markets (long) AND falling markets (short)</li>
              <li><strong>No Commissions:</strong> Many brokers offer commission-free trading (only the spread)</li>
              <li><strong>Small Account Friendly:</strong> Start with as little as $50-100 with micro/nano lots</li>
              <li><strong>Transparent Pricing:</strong> Real-time prices from multiple liquidity providers</li>
              <li><strong>No Insider Trading:</strong> Economic data is released to everyone simultaneously</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          
          <div class="cons-card">
            <div class="pros-cons-header">❌ Disadvantages of Forex Trading</div>
            <ul class="pros-cons-list">
              <li><strong>High Risk of Loss:</strong> Most retail traders lose money. Leverage amplifies losses as much as gains</li>
              <li><strong>Complex Market:</strong> Many factors influence prices (economic, political, psychological, technical)</li>
              <li><strong>Broker Risk:</strong> Unregulated brokers can manipulate prices, refuse withdrawals, or go bankrupt</li>
              <li><strong>Gap Risk:</strong> Prices can gap past stop losses during weekends or news events</li>
              <li><strong>Emotional Trading:</strong> High leverage and fast moves trigger fear and greed, leading to poor decisions</li>
              <li><strong>Scams & Fraud:</strong> Many "signal sellers," robots, and courses are scams preying on beginners</li>
              <li><strong>News Volatility:</strong> Economic releases cause unpredictable spikes and slippage</li>
              <li><strong>Negative Swap:</strong> Holding positions overnight may cost interest if you're on the wrong side</li>
              <li><strong>Requires Constant Learning:</strong> Markets change, strategies become obsolete, need continuous education</li>
              <li><strong>Time Consuming:</strong> Successful trading requires hours of analysis, journaling, and screen time</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

